WI-solob. The previous attempt could never have worked, whatever the
credentials: it built the logs path from the UI url's trailing number, and
that number is the WORKFLOW INDEX, not the step. The logs endpoint keys on
the step's GLOBAL id. Measured against the live instance:

  /api/repos/1/logs/290/9      (pid)      -> 404
  /api/repos/1/logs/290/1653   (step id)  -> 200, 116 KB

So a fetch is two calls. Resolve the step from
  GET /api/repos/{repo}/pipelines/{number}
then fetch
  GET /api/repos/{repo}/logs/{number}/{step_id}

Step selection prefers a step whose name matches the requested job, and
otherwise picks the FAILED step, so `ci-debug logs` with no arguments lands
on whatever actually broke instead of the first step.

Two decoding details, both taken from a real response rather than guessed,
and both of which silently corrupt output if assumed away: entries can carry
`data: null` (naive joining raises TypeError), and each entry is ONE line
carrying a `line` ordinal, so they must be sorted by it and joined with
newlines rather than concatenated.

BEHAVIORAL EVIDENCE, end to end on the live instance:
  `ci-debug logs "" 6435792229c5`               -> auto-picks the failed step
                                                   and ends with the real
                                                   ERROR from PR #92
  `ci-debug logs verify-generated 6435792229c5` -> same ERROR
  `ci-debug logs pytest 6435792229c5`           -> a DIFFERENT step's log
                                                   (TOTAL 183 0 100%), which
                                                   proves selection actually
                                                   discriminates rather than
                                                   always returning the failure

The fallback also gains a 404 line, since "past both gates but wrong path" is
now a distinguishable state and was exactly the one that bit here.

